A||||6||Fformula88||HEADLIGHT HELP!!!||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||09-27-2000||08:16 PM||||On my dads 87 Coupe, there is something wrong with the electrical that runs the headlight motors.  The LH motor (drivers side) continues to run after its all the way up, and all the way down.  The left is fine.  It sounds to me like the control module may be bad.  The switch is fine.  I am workin on it now, so any advice is GREATLY appreciated.  Thanx.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/Motorcity/Boulevard/9278/fierosig1.jpg">   <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 09-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||AL||09-27-2000||08:31 PM||||it sounds like the gear is spinning, take it apart and look at the gear if it's stripped replace it.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-27-2000||08:32 PM||||Not an electrical problem.<P>repair the pins in the motors. get the parts & instructions to do both motors for $10 from <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  (If one motor is doing this the other likely isn't far behind.<P>For the time being unplug the motors from the harness and leave the lights up. The connectors are right by the motors.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||09-27-2000||09:15 PM||||I have a new motor and it does the same thing.  So its not the motor.  The biggest sign of a problem is when I put just the parking lights on and then turn them off the motor will start to spin.  I have also hooked the control mod up to my Formula and it is fine.  could be a short somewhere else.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||09-27-2000||09:23 PM||||I just got my kit from Rodney to fix the headlights on my 88. They have been eternally locked up for ages with the motors unplugged. Who knew I could fix it for a mere $10...<P>I do dread having to take the things completely apart to install the repair kits. Anyone that has done this have any helpful tips to make it easier?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||09-27-2000||10:12 PM||||ok, I got it all workin  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I cleaned all the contacts and put it all together and hoped for the best.  Thankfully it worked.  I am planning on rebuilding the old one as a spare in case I loose another motor.  Thanks for all the quick advise everyone!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-27-2000||11:28 PM||||The only way a gen 2 motor can keep spinning is if the pins are shot or the gear is stripped. (Or if some dolt left the linkage loose on the shaft and it's gotten wrecked.)<P><BR>The drivers motor is easy. The pasengers one is a bit of a pain. Follow the directions in the kit.<P>One Note: If the shaft hangs up as you try to slide off the cover or pull the shaft from the motor then STOP PULLING!<P>If the shaft binds comming out then the shaft is likely flared a little on the shoulders of the flat section that the arm was held on. You have to file off the rolled edges or you will damage the shaft bearings in the motor or cover.<P>Use "white" lithium grease, Lubriplate, or the silicone grease for brake work to lub the parts. Those are both more plastic friendly than most regular greases used in cars etc.<P>DO NOT get grease or oil on the brushes or armature if you take the motor itself apart. Clean the electrical contacts with brake cleaner.||||reg||1
